{
  "image_name": "ae63cb245d423deb3970e092d8cb710f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure brings together several examples of how solid electrolytes or related compounds react and form interphases at electrochemical interfaces. Overall, it emphasizes that stability depends on voltage, chemical potentials, and interfacial mixing.\n\nOne part shows voltage-dependent phase stability for a lithium thiophosphate system: as the potential changes from 0 V to higher values, the favored composition shifts from simple binary sulfide/phosphide phases toward several intermediate lithium–phosphorus–sulfur compounds, indicating decomposition products can vary strongly with electrochemical conditions.\n\nAnother panel illustrates sodium defects in a selenide framework, highlighting an extraction voltage near 2.75 V. This suggests sodium can be removed from the host lattice, leaving a vacancy and reflecting oxidative stability limits.\n\nA schematic interface model shows two neighboring materials, such as an electrode and a solid electrolyte, in contact with external Li and O reservoirs. Near the interface, atoms exchange and a thin mixed layer forms with composition between those of the two bulk materials, conveying the idea of a chemically equilibrated reaction zone.\n\nThe final atomistic image shows the LLZO/Li metal interface, where the crystalline oxide and lithium metal meet directly, visually reinforcing the complex, atom-by-atom nature of these buried solid-state interfaces.",
